            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Theresa"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Excellent beans! I thought next time I'd add one more can of beans though.. and I only used a quarter of a small container of those french fried onions - there's no way I could have put 6 ounces on them!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Joy Berger"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I tripled the recipe and used a 6 oz. Can of French onion rings on top. I wasn't thinking and put the onion rings on right away. So I just covered it during the initial heating then took off the lid during the last 15 minutes. It was perfect.",
            "datePublished": "2019-04-21T21:54:00.937Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "AutumnRose"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Definitely a good starter recipe! I usually double the amount of green beans, mix in half the can of fried onions and top with the other half. I also mix in some shredded, sharp cheddar cheese. Have gotten many compliments on it!",
            "datePublished": "2012-07-29T03:56:23.217Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Margie"
            },
            "reviewBody": "In two weeks made these already twice, everyone loved them, making them the second time I used 1 and 1/2 cans of creamed celery soup instead of the creamed mushroom, a tablespoon of melted butter in a less than quarter cup of water, mixed the soup, onions and fresh beans all together before baking",
            "datePublished": "2010-01-01T19:33:23.673Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "DTNYC"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I served this for Christmas dinner and my family enjoyed it. I used 2 cans of beans and added some grated cheddar cheese and a couple tablespoons of milk. My personal preference is to mix the can of onions into the casserole right before I put it in the oven, rather than putting the onions on top. My sister-in-law made it one year for Thanksgiving by adding a can of creamed corn and it was surprisingly very good.",
            "datePublished": "2003-01-02T16:54:23.390Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CHORT1313"
            },
            "reviewBody": "thanks for sharing this classic! I used fresh green beans and had to cook them for about 40 mins. I also mixed up some of the onions (from someone's suggestion) and put some on top. Delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2003-10-14T07:53:03.680Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SnappyKelp6806"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Frozen french-cut (on the bias) green beans are even better than canned. Let them come to room temperature before proceeding.",
            "datePublished": "2024-11-14T22:38:01.992Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Erin"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It’s very good. I don’t know who Pearl is, but my G’ma made this in the 60’s - It’s a very old recipe that I use every year. My family doesn’t like mushrooms so I use cream of celery, or any other creamed soup, and it needs pepper.",
            "datePublished": "2025-11-19T14:30:43.477Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This recipe is very close to the version I make. For variation I sometimes mix chow mein noodles in the casserole and some on top for a crunchier casserole (instead of french fried onions). I also add 1 TB soy sauce and 3 TB sliced and scattered butter on top. Yum",
            "datePublished": "2002-11-23T17:15:30.467Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jojosmama1204"
            },
            "reviewBody": "For our family thanksgiving, I made a triple batch and added an extra can of green beans. I think the 1:1 ratio is a little heavy on the soup. Overall, this is a perfect base recipe to start from though!",
            "datePublished": "2018-11-25T16:59:36.587Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
